<strong>Open plan kitchen renovation cost</strong> is one of the first questions homeowners ask when considering a major home improvement. While creating an open-plan kitchen can completely transform the way you live, entertain and use your home, the final cost depends on much more than simply removing a wall. Discovering inventive <a href="https://www.oraanj-interiors.co.uk/open-plan-kitchen-living-ideas-creating-a-stylish-functional-space/">open plan kitchen living ideas</a> helps align layout aspirations with realistic expenditure.

Whether you're opening up a Victorian terrace, extending a family home or modernising an outdated layout, understanding where your budget goes will help you make informed decisions and avoid unexpected expenses.
<h2>How Much Does an Open Plan Kitchen Renovation Cost?</h2>
<img class="alignnone wp-image-12938 size-full" src="https://www.oraanj-interiors.co.uk/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/muswell-hill-north-london-edwardian-kitchen-dining-living-interior.webp" alt="" width="1672" height="941" />

There isn't a single answer because every property is different.

A straightforward renovation where the existing layout remains largely unchanged will naturally cost less than a project involving structural alterations, steel beams, plumbing relocation, new electrics and bespoke cabinetry.

As a general guide:
<ul>
 	<li><strong>Basic open-plan kitchen renovation:</strong> £20,000–£35,000</li>
 	<li><strong>Mid-range renovation:</strong> £35,000–£60,000</li>
 	<li><strong>Luxury open-plan kitchen renovation:</strong> £60,000+</li>
</ul>
The biggest influence on cost is usually the amount of structural work required rather than the kitchen units themselves, though investing in <a href="https://www.oraanj-interiors.co.uk/bespoke-joinery-design-london/">custom kitchen cabinetry and bespoke joinery</a> ensures long-term durability and spatial efficiency.
<h2>Structural Changes Often Take the Largest Share of the Budget</h2>
<img class="alignnone wp-image-12997 size-full" src="https://www.oraanj-interiors.co.uk/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/kitchen-renovation-pre-construction-stage.webp" alt="" width="1254" height="1254" />

Many homeowners assume cabinetry is the most expensive part of an open-plan kitchen.

In reality, removing walls, installing steel beams (RSJs), altering drainage, relocating services and strengthening floors can account for a significant proportion of the overall budget.

Before choosing worktops or appliances, it's important to understand whether the wall you want to remove is load-bearing. If it is, structural calculations and additional construction work will be required.

This is why working with an experienced <a href="https://www.oraanj-interiors.co.uk/renovation-company-london/"><strong>renovation company in London</strong></a> can help identify potential challenges before construction begins.
<h2>Your Kitchen Layout Will Affect the Cost</h2>
The size and complexity of your new kitchen also influence the overall investment.

For example:
<ul>
 	<li>A galley kitchen opened into a dining room usually requires less work than combining several rooms.</li>
 	<li>Adding a large kitchen island increases cabinetry, worktop and electrical costs.</li>
 	<li>Moving plumbing for sinks or appliances can significantly increase labour costs.</li>
 	<li>Installing bi-fold or sliding doors may require additional structural work.</li>
</ul>
Good planning often saves more money than choosing cheaper finishes. Even when working with compact footprints, reviewing <a href="https://www.oraanj-interiors.co.uk/tiny-kitchen-ideas-smart-stylish-space-saving-solutions-for-small-homes/">tiny kitchen ideas for small homes</a> reveals how smart zoning keeps build costs manageable.
<h2>Don't Forget the Hidden Costs Of Kitchen Renovation</h2>
